Fixes #19960

## Issue

When an empty CSS file is used as a rollup entry point, Vite incorrectly generates an empty JS file instead of an empty CSS file. This causes problems for build integrations that read the manifest and expect CSS entries to produce CSS assets.

**Before:**
```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    manifest: true,
    rollupOptions: {
      input: ['empty.css'] // Completely empty CSS file
    }
  }
}
```

Generated manifest:
```json
{
  "empty.css": {
    "file": "assets/empty-l0sNRNKZ.js", // ❌ JS file generated
    "name": "empty",
    "src": "empty.css",
    "isEntry": true
  }
}
```

**After:**
```json
{
  "empty.css": {
    "file": "assets/empty-tn0RQdqM.css", // ✅ CSS file generated
    "src": "empty.css", 
    "isEntry": true
  }
}
```

## Root Cause

In `packages/vite/src/node/plugins/css.ts`, the condition `if (chunkCSS)` excluded empty CSS files from CSS asset emission. Since completely empty CSS files result in an empty `chunkCSS` string (falsy), they bypassed the CSS processing logic and were handled as regular JS chunks instead.

## Solution

Modified the chunkCSS initialization and logic to better distinguish between "no CSS processed" vs "processed but empty":

```typescript
// Before
let chunkCSS = ''
if (chunkCSS) {
  // CSS processing logic
}

// After  
let chunkCSS: string | undefined = undefined
if (chunkCSS !== undefined) {
  // CSS processing logic
}
```

This ensures that CSS entry points are always processed through the CSS pipeline and emit CSS assets, regardless of whether their content is empty after processing/minification.

## Testing

Added comprehensive E2E test covering empty CSS files in the main CSS test suite. Verified all existing CSS tests continue to pass and confirmed fix works for CSS files that become empty after minification.

The fix is minimal and surgical, addressing the specific edge case while preserving all existing CSS functionality.

